# gzip 1.2.4, compiled by the tcc this chain built, against ntlibc.
#
# The PE32 counterpart of ../../../linux/bootstrap/gzip/build.sh, which drives
# gzip's own ./configure under bash.  There is no configure here.  gzip 1.2.4
# is a 1993 program with no config.h and no gnulib: everything configure
# decides it hands to the compiler as -D, and the whole list is documented at
# the top of gzip's own Makefile.in.  So the -D wall below IS the configure
# run, the way gnugrep and gnupatch do it, and the build is fourteen commands
# that kaem can name directly.  That also keeps gzip off make, which on this
# side loses roughly one child in a hundred and fifty to a bad exec.
#
# ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}, not ${TMPDIR}: wine drops TMPDIR on the way into the
# Windows environment, so it arrives empty and cd is left with no argument.
#
#
# WIN32, _WIN32 and MSDOS
# -----------------------
# gzip is the first package on this side with genuine DOS/Windows support of
# its own, so this needed deciding rather than inheriting.  Measured first:
# this tcc predefines _WIN32 and NOTHING ELSE of interest -- not WIN32, not
# MSDOS, not unix, not even __STDC__.  gzip's tailor.h tests bare `WIN32' and
# bare `MSDOS'; the string `_WIN32' does not appear anywhere in the gzip
# sources.  So no -U is needed, and none is passed: coreutils had to take
# _WIN32 away because gnulib reads it as "the Win32 API is here", but nothing
# in gzip ever looks at it.
#
# Defining WIN32 would have been the tempting move and it is the wrong one.
# tailor.h's `#ifdef WIN32' block is a block about the Microsoft C runtime,
# not about the operating system: it includes <io.h>, <malloc.h> and
# <sys/utime.h>, none of which ntlibc has, and it defines SET_BINARY_MODE as
# setmode(fd, O_BINARY), which ntlibc has no setmode for -- and would not need
# one, because ntlibc is the NT system-call layer and does no CRLF translation
# in the first place.  There is no text mode here to escape from.  Verified by
# round-tripping two megabytes of /dev/urandom through `gzip -c' and
# `gzip -dc' as a pipe and getting the same sha256 back.
#
# Three further things follow from leaving WIN32 undefined, and all three are
# the answer this port wants:
#
#   OS_CODE stays 0x03, "Unix", the value tailor.h falls back to -- rather
#   than 0x0b, "NTFS".  This is the byte at offset 9 of every gzip header this
#   program writes, so it is real archive metadata and not a detail.  0x03 is
#   the honest answer: what this gzip is running on is a POSIX surface
#   (utime, chown, lstat, opendir, all of them real ntlibc calls), not the
#   Win32 file API that 0x0b describes.  It also means a .gz produced here and
#   one produced by the Linux half of this bootstrap are byte-identical, which
#   is worth having in a bootstrap that builds the same sources twice.
#   Checked against the host's own gzip: same sixteen header bytes, same OS
#   byte, same stored name, same mtime.
#
#   Z_SUFFIX stays ".gz" and NO_MULTIPLE_DOTS stays undefined, so names are
#   not truncated to 8.3 and `foo.tar.gz' is a name this gzip will make.  The
#   WIN32 block only forces those under NTFAT, which is a FAT filesystem, not
#   this one.
#
#   NO_CHOWN stays undefined, so gzip calls chown() to copy ownership.  ntlibc
#   has chown; gzip ignores its return value, so a failure on a filesystem
#   with no ownership to copy is not an error here any more than it is on
#   Linux.
#
# What IS wanted out of that block is PATH_SEP2 and PATH_SEP3, and those are
# in config.h -- see the head of that file for why they are load-bearing and
# why they cannot be -D on a kaem command line.
#
# One more define arrives by omission rather than by choice: tailor.h ends
# with `#ifndef unix / #define NO_ST_INO', and this tcc does not define unix.
# So gzip does not trust st_ino to tell whether the input and output files are
# the same, and falls back to comparing names.  That is the right answer for a
# library whose st_ino comes from an NT file id that need not be stable.

cd ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}

${bin_ungz} --file ${tarball} --output gzip.tar
${bin_untar} --file gzip.tar

# Everything is built in the source directory, so that gzip's quoted includes
# -- "tailor.h", "gzip.h", "lzw.h", "crypt.h" -- are found by -I. ; this tcc
# does not search an included file's own directory.
cd ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gzip-${version}

${bin_cp} ${configH} config.h

# The four answers a ./configure would have reached on this system, and
# nothing else.  Every one of them is a plain "the library has this":
#
#   STDC_HEADERS    gzip.h reads it as "<string.h> and <stdlib.h> exist".
#                   Without it gzip.h includes <strings.h> INSTEAD and defines
#                   strchr to index, memcpy to bcopy and memzero to bzero --
#                   the same trap sed met from the other direction.  ntlibc's
#                   <strings.h> has bcopy and bzero, so this would even link;
#                   it would just be the 1980s spelling of a library that has
#                   the real one.
#   HAVE_UNISTD_H   ntlibc has it, and util.c and gzip.c want read/write/close
#                   declared.
#   DIRENT          picks <dirent.h> for the -r recursion, over the three
#                   pre-POSIX spellings gzip also knows.  ntlibc has opendir,
#                   readdir and closedir.  Verified by running `gzip -r' over
#                   a two-level tree.
#   VERSION/REVDATE are not passed: revision.h carries them, unlike sed.
#
# Four things live-bootstrap's Linux recipe does that are NOT done here:
#
#   -Dstrlwr=unused is dropped.  gzip DEFINES strlwr in util.c, so that -D
#                   renames the definition and its three call sites together;
#                   it is there because Mes's C library declares a strlwr of
#                   its own that the definition then contradicts.  ntlibc has
#                   no strlwr at all -- checked with nm over libc.a -- so
#                   there is nothing to collide with and gzip's own is used.
#                   This is the usual shape: the Linux workaround would have
#                   been carried here for a problem this side does not have.
#   ./configure     is not run.  See the head of this file.
#   the clock tick  the Linux script busy-waits for a second before running
#                   configure, because untar restores no mtimes there and
#                   configure compares them.  Nothing here compares them.
#   match.S         is not assembled.  configure adds -DASMV and match.o when
#                   it can assemble gzip's hand-written i386 longest_match;
#                   that file is AT&T syntax for gas, this chain has no gas,
#                   and deflate.c's C longest_match is what is compiled
#                   instead.  It is slower and it is identical in output.
#
# A PE-target tcc adds neither a startup object nor a default C library, and
# the default it would reach for (msvcrt, kernel32) is not what this bootstrap
# has, so crt1.o and -lc/-lntdll are named outright -- ntdll.def being the
# import library ntlibc resolves its kernel calls through.  The Linux build's
# -static has no meaning here.
alias CC="${tcc} -c -B ${ntlibc}/lib -I. -nostdinc -I${ntlibcSrc}/include -I${ntlibcSrc}/arch/i386 -I${ntlibcSrc}/arch/generic -I${ntlibc}/include -include config.h -DSTDC_HEADERS=1 -DHAVE_UNISTD_H=1 -DDIRENT=1"

alias LINK="${tcc} -B ${ntlibc}/lib -nostdlib ${ntlibc}/lib/crt1.o"

CC -o gzip.o gzip.c
CC -o zip.o zip.c
CC -o deflate.o deflate.c
CC -o trees.o trees.c
CC -o bits.o bits.c
CC -o unzip.o unzip.c
CC -o inflate.o inflate.c
CC -o util.o util.c
CC -o crypt.o crypt.c
CC -o lzw.o lzw.c
CC -o unlzw.o unlzw.c
CC -o unpack.o unpack.c
CC -o unlzh.o unlzh.c

# getopt.o is the fourteenth object the Makefile builds and it is not built
# here, for the reason gnugrep drops the same two files: ntlibc has getopt,
# getopt_long and getopt_long_only, and its struct option has the same four
# members in the same order that gzip's own getopt.h declares -- so gzip's
# header still describes what it links against, and building gzip's copy would
# make this linker, which resolves archives in one pass, see the symbol
# defined twice.
#
# The collision that goes the OTHER way is left alone deliberately.  util.c
# defines basename(), and ntlibc has a basename() too, in its own libc.a
# member.  gzip's objects come first on the link line, so gzip's definition
# wins and basename.o is never pulled in -- which is what is wanted: gzip's
# basename strips a version suffix and lowercases on case-folding systems,
# and POSIX basename does neither.  It is silent rather than an error, which
# is exactly why it is written down here.
#
# -lc -lntdll come last, after gzip's own objects, which is what a linker
# that resolves archives in one pass needs.
LINK -o gzip.exe gzip.o zip.o deflate.o trees.o bits.o unzip.o inflate.o util.o crypt.o lzw.o unlzw.o unpack.o unlzh.o -L ${ntlibc}/lib -lc -lntdll

# Run it before installing it, and on real data rather than --version.
#
# The strong check is `-t' on gzip's own release tarball: half a megabyte of
# deflate stream produced by some other gzip years ago, whose CRC32 and length
# trailer this inflate has to reproduce exactly.  Then the same check on this
# gzip's own output, over the 700K tar the unpacker left lying about, which is
# large enough to run the 32K window and the whole Huffman path rather than a
# stored block.  A CRC mismatch either way is a non-zero exit and kaem is
# --strict, so the build fails.
#
# The cases that cannot be run here are run outside and reported in
# default.nix: pipes and redirection (kaem has neither), and the deliberate
# failures -- a corrupted file, a bad magic number -- which exit 1 on purpose.
cd ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}
${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gzip-${version}/gzip.exe --version
${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gzip-${version}/gzip.exe -t ${tarball}
${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gzip-${version}/gzip.exe -9 gzip.tar
${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gzip-${version}/gzip.exe -t gzip.tar.gz
${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gzip-${version}/gzip.exe -d gzip.tar.gz

# gunzip and zcat are the same program under two more names.  On Unix the
# makefile's install target makes them hard links; there are none to make
# here, so they are copies -- three times 150K, which is not worth a
# indirection this bootstrap would have to invent.  gzip works out which of
# the three it is from argv[0], which is why config.h exists at all.
#
# All three keep the .exe suffix, as bash, make, patch, sed and grep do --
# ntlibc's PATH search tries the bare name before name.exe, so a script asking
# for `gzip' still finds it.  gzip itself strips a trailing ".exe" from
# progname before deciding its mode, so gunzip.exe is gunzip.
${bin_mkdir} -p ${out}/bin
${bin_cp} ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gzip-${version}/gzip.exe ${out}/bin/gzip.exe
${bin_cp} ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gzip-${version}/gzip.exe ${out}/bin/gunzip.exe
${bin_cp} ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gzip-${version}/gzip.exe ${out}/bin/zcat.exe
